.TH SHUF 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
shuf \- write the lines of its input in random order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shuf
.RB [ \-z ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RI [ infile ]
.br
.B shuf \-e
.RB [ \-z ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.RI [ arg ...]
.br
.B shuf \-i
.IR low \- high
.RB [ \-z ]
.RB [ \-n
.IR count ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR outfile ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shuf
reads lines, puts them in random order, and writes them.
The lines come from
.IR infile ,
or from standard input when there is no
.I infile
or it is
.BR \- .
With
.BR \-e ,
they are the
.I arg
operands instead, and with
.BR \-i ,
the integers from
.I low
to
.IR high .
.PP
With
.BR \-n ,
.B shuf
writes at most
.I count
lines, chosen at random, with no input line chosen twice.
.PP
All the input is read before any output is written.
A line ends at an LF or at a NUL byte, and the terminator is removed
from the line, as is a CR before an LF.
The last line needs no terminator, and an empty line is a line.
Empty input has no lines and produces no output.
.PP
Each output line is followed by an LF, or by a NUL with
.BR \-z ,
including the last.
With
.BR \-i ,
the lines are numbers in decimal without leading zeros.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-zn3 ,
and may follow operands.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-n3 ,
or be the next argument.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
A repeated option takes the last value.
.TP
.BI "\-n " count
Write at most
.I count
lines.
The default is all of them.
.I count
is a decimal number from 0 to 4294967295, with no sign, space, or
suffix.
.TP
.BI "\-o " outfile
Write to
.I outfile
instead of standard output.
It is created if it does not exist and truncated if it does, after the
input has been read and shuffled, so it may name
.I infile
to shuffle a file in place.
It is created even when nothing is written, but not when an earlier
error ends the command.
A name of
.B \-
creates a file of that name, and does not mean standard output.
.TP
.B \-z
Terminate each output line with a NUL byte instead of an LF.
The input is read as described in
.BR DESCRIPTION ,
whether or not
.B \-z
is given.
.TP
.B \-e
Take the lines from the
.I arg
operands, one line each, and read no input.
An operand that begins with
.BR \- ,
other than
.B \-
itself, is taken as an option unless
.B \-\-
comes first.
With no operands there are no lines.
Incompatible with
.BR \-i .
.TP
.BI "\-i " low \- high
Take the lines from the integers
.I low
through
.IR high ,
inclusive.
Each of the two is a decimal number from 0 to 18446744073709551615, of
digits only, with no sign, space, suffix, or
.B 0x
prefix.
A range that is malformed, has
.I high
below
.IR low ,
or holds too many numbers is an error.
A range holds at most 4294967295 numbers, or 1073741823 in the 32-bit
kit.
No other operand is allowed.
The whole range is held in memory, 8 bytes per number (4 in the 32-bit
kit), even when
.B \-n
selects a few.
Incompatible with
.BR \-e .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the arguments are expanded when
.B shuf
starts, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
This includes the
.I arg
operands of
.BR \-e .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
arguments as the shell expanded them.
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
When it has the owner write bit,
.BR 200 ,
the file that
.B \-o
creates is read-only, so a later
.B shuf \-o
cannot replace it.
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_CTYPE ", " LANG
If the first of these that is set has the value
.BR C ,
output to a console is not converted from the ANSI code page, and the
console interprets the bytes in its own code page.
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the C library ends option parsing at the first argument that
is not an option, so later arguments such as
.B \-n
are operands.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet, such as
.B BB_TERMINAL_MODE
for console output, are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Error.
An unknown option, a missing option argument, both
.B \-e
and
.BR \-i ,
or operands that do not fit the form, namely a second
.I infile
or any operand with
.BR \-i ,
write a usage message on standard error.
Any other error, such as an invalid
.I count
or range, an
.I infile
or
.I outfile
that cannot be opened, or a failure writing output, is reported on
standard error in one line that begins with
.BR shuf: .
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
Output is written in 4096-byte blocks, and a closed pipe found when the
last block is written at exit, as when the output is 4096 bytes or less,
is instead an error with status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
Shuffle the lines of a file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shuf playlist.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Choose one line at random:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shuf \-n 1 quotes.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
Roll a six-sided die:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shuf \-i 1\-6 \-n 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Choose three of the words given as arguments:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shuf \-e red green blue amber \-n 3
.EE
.RE
.PP
Shuffle a file in place:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ shuf \-o playlist.txt playlist.txt
.EE
.RE
.PP
List ten files chosen at random.
The NUL terminators keep names that contain spaces intact:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.png\(aq \-print0 | shuf \-z \-n 10 | xargs \-0 ls \-l
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Differences from GNU
.BR shuf :
.IP \(bu 2
There are no long options other than
.BR \-\-help ,
no
.BR \-\-version ,
and no
.B \-r
or
.BR \-\-random\-source .
.IP \(bu 2
Input lines end at an LF or a NUL, with or without
.BR \-z ,
which changes only the output.
GNU
.B shuf
ends input lines only at an LF, or with
.B \-z
only at a NUL.
.IP \(bu 2
The numbers for
.B \-n
and
.B \-i
may not have a leading sign or space, which GNU
.B shuf
accepts.
.PP
The shuffle is a Fisher-Yates shuffle driven by the C library's
.BR rand ,
seeded from the system time in microseconds.
It is not suitable for security use, and it cannot be seeded or
repeated.
Each swap partner is chosen as a random value modulo the number of lines
left.
The value has 15 bits while at most 32767 lines are left, and 30 bits
otherwise, so the choice is not uniform.
For example, from 20000 lines,
.B shuf \-n 1
picks each of the first 12768 twice as often as each of the others, and
from more than 1073741824 lines it never picks a line past that number.
.PP
All the lines are held in memory, each with a pointer, so an input that
never ends never produces output, and a very large one fails with an
out-of-memory error.
.PP
The output has LF line endings, and a CR before an LF is removed from
each line, so
.B shuf
converts text with Windows line endings to Unix line endings.
A lone CR is not a line break.
Except on a console, other bytes, including Ctrl-Z, pass through
unchanged.
To end input typed at a console, type Ctrl-Z at the start of a line and
press Enter.
Output to a console has no
.B \-z
terminators, so the lines run together.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and backslashes.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The device names
.I /dev/null
and
.I /dev/stdin
work for
.IR infile ,
and
.I /dev/null
and
.I /dev/stdout
for
.IR outfile .
A directory as
.I infile
fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.PP
When standard output is a console, text is converted for display from
the ANSI code page, which is UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 and later,
as described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR head (1),
.BR seq (1),
.BR sort (1),
.BR xargs (1)
